# Third-party notices

`pydasm` is MIT licensed (see `LICENSE`). It is a port, and it incorporates
material and derives behaviour from work by others. This file records what, and
what is deliberately **not** in this repository.

## Ported from marty_dasm

The decoder is a port of [marty_dasm](https://github.com/dbalsom/marty_dasm) by
Daniel Balsom:

- `src/pydasm/i808x/decode.py`, `modrm16.py`, `operands.py`, `gdr.py` —
  the 8088/8086 decoder
- `src/pydasm/nasm_formatter.py` — the NASM-style renderer
- `src/pydasm/i808x/decode_table.py`, `src/pydasm/_mnemonics.py` — generated
  from the crate's Rust source by `tools/gen_decode_table.py` and
  `tools/gen_mnemonics.py`
- `src/pydasm/cpu_common.py`, `byte_reader.py`, `instruction.py` — the shared
  data model

MIT licensed. The `capture_nasm_oracle.sh` script builds a small driver inside a
marty_dasm checkout to capture the reference formatter's output as a test
oracle; that driver is written by this project and removed with the checkout.

## Hardware test recordings

`tests/data/sst_8088.json` and `tests/data/sst_8086.json` are samples of the
[SingleStepTests](https://github.com/SingleStepTests) suites, MIT licensed.
They are recordings of an AMD D8088 and an Intel P80C86A, made by Daniel Balsom
with Arduino8088/ArduinoX86 and MartyPC. The samples were produced by
`tools/check_sst.py --make-corpus`; the full suites are not redistributed here.

## Not included: the binary corpus

`tests/data/` deliberately does **not** contain the third-party DOS binary the
Ghidra corpus was taken over, nor the Ghidra listing itself, nor the Java
exporter that produced it.

**Why the listing is excluded, not just the binary.** A byte-anchored Ghidra
listing carries the bytes it decoded. The listing this project was developed
against reproduced 74,394 of the 86,192 load-image bytes — 86% — including the
program's own text and copyright strings. Distributing it would republish the
binary just as surely as distributing the file.

Four tests need that corpus (`tests/test_ghidra_corpus.py`, `test_mz.py`,
`test_frame.py`, and part of `test_cli.py` and `test_segments.py`). They are
gated on the `corpus` fixture and **skip** when it is absent, so a clone runs
green without it. To run them, point `PYDASM_CORPUS` at a directory containing:

| File | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| `target.EXE` | the image, obtained by you, under whatever terms apply to it |
| `listing.txt` | a listing for it in the format described in `docs/hardware-tests.md` |
| `segments.json` | its load layout, from `tools/gen_segments.py` |

The exporter that produces such a listing belongs to the project this was
developed alongside. It is that project's file, it is not licensed for
redistribution here, and it is therefore not included — pass your own to
`tools/capture_ghidra_listing.sh`. The format it emits is documented in
`docs/hardware-tests.md`.

Nothing else in the repository depends on the corpus: 177 of 211 tests run
without it.

## Ghidra

`pydasm.ghidra` reproduces the *output format* of a byte-anchored Ghidra
exporter. It contains no Ghidra code. The formatting rules were derived by
comparing against captured output, and the immediate signedness rule was checked
against the x86 SLEIGH specification shipped with Ghidra
(`Ghidra/Processors/x86/data/languages/ia.sinc`, Apache 2.0) — cited in
`src/pydasm/ghidra.py`, not copied.
